Chapter 10: "Precipitation of Trust"

"So, in the end, even from the boss's point of view, you couldn't tell which one—that Filioare 'Saint' or Lady Felt—was the real missing royal, huh? Here."

*clink*

"But you saw that Duke Karsten's health recovered afterward, right? Whether she's royalty or not, the power of the sacred rites is real, isn't it? Here."

*nod*

"Yeah, you're right. I think Beatrice and Rem, who went with me, would vouch for that too. What Emilia-tan and the others saw was definitely real. ...And honestly, I couldn't deny that Filioare came across as a super good kid either. She seemed like the Emilia-tan type, no ill will or anything. Mm."

*thump*

Letting out a deep sigh, Subaru recalled the rumored 'Saint' he had come face to face with at the church that day. With an indescribable feeling, he placed the plate in his hands onto the shelf.

The cupboard in the Royal Palace Guest House was stocked with numerous dishes, both wooden and ceramic. Subaru and the others were in the middle of washing, drying, and putting away the dishes they had used for their meal. At the water source, Garfiel was washing plates, Petra was drying them, and Subaru was putting them away in that order.

The Royal Palace Guest House was essentially a guesthouse for high-ranking nobles with business in the royal capital. Consequently, it was thoroughly equipped, with thoughtful amenities always provided to ensure no inconvenience. Apparently, if needed, housekeepers could be dispatched to handle cooking, cleaning, and personal care, but most nobles brought along trusted servants, so their services were seldom used.

The Emilia camp had also had Petra handle today's meal preparations, and for cleanup, Subaru and Garfiel—who had lost at rock-paper-scissors—were cooperating.

And so, during their coordinated cleanup among the three of them, the conversation had drifted to the topic at hand.

"...But if even you guys say there's no mistake about it from what you saw, then honestly, my real feeling is that I want her to somehow fix the problems in Pristera."

"Yeah, but what if it turns out that this Filioare person is the real royalty? Then Emilia-nee-sama's royal selection might end, you know?"

"Well, that's a whole different story, innit? The thing is, this 'Saint' Filioare has already shown herself on the public stage. It's just that only the bigwigs know about it so far."

Responding to Petra's words while scrubbing dishes, Garfiel created soap suds in the bucket of water with a vigorous splash. Squinting his emerald-green eyes, he wrestled with stubborn stains on a plate using a linen cloth as a sponge.

"I ain't seen her directly myself, but if she's the kind of person who'd cause such a stir just by her looks, there's no way she can stay hidden even if they try to hide her. Especially if she's gonna heal the Duke's body and do the same thing in Pristera."

"――Hmm, I think Garf-san's understanding there is spot on. You're getting better at reading the social situation. Good job, good job."

"Don't talk to me like I'm takin' some test! And for the record, I'm older than Petra, ya know!"

Even as Petra, looking like a teacher, patted his head, Garfiel, with both hands plunged into the bucket, couldn't resist and could only grumble in protest. Watching their exchange warmly, Subaru couldn't help but sense an unpleasant turn in the public mood.

"At this point, it feels like the royal selection's development is showing a yellow light..."

If this were to turn into a red light from here, would it ever be able to switch back to green, allowing Emilia to properly fulfill her role as a royal selection candidate?

"――――"

Honestly, Subaru was concerned that from the moment the possibility of two surviving princesses—Felt and Filioare—emerged, the royal selection might have shifted into something seeking the answer to 'which one is the real one with the legitimate succession rights?'

As Emilia's knight, that was naturally unacceptable, but even setting aside that perspective, there was something he couldn't accept.

It was something that had come up even during the journey to the royal capital via Reinhard's high-speed travel method.

"Come to think of it, even before Filioare showed up, when the suspicion arose about Felt being a princess, why didn't things just flow towards Felt being decided as the next king?"

Of course, there's no doubt this world lacks convenient and reliable materials for identifying individuals, like DNA tests, fingerprint registration, or dental records.

But the reason for the suspicion cast on Felt as a princess was also based on rather vague factors: her rare physical characteristics and the fact that her age matched that of the missing princess. If that were sufficient proof of lineage in this world, the royal selection could have ended that day without any awkwardness for Subaru at the royal castle.

"But it didn't end like that. The primary reason for that is――"

"――The Dragon Chronicle."

Raising an eyebrow at having the conclusion stolen, Subaru saw Petra, who had just finished drying a plate handed to her by Garfiel, looking at him while holding out that very plate.

Subaru nodded, saying "Yeah," and took both the plate and that conclusion from her.

"It's a prophecy tablet that's supposedly helped the kingdom out in various tough spots throughout history... I hear that Dragon Chronicle gave the order to hold the royal selection, but..."

"What's with that tone? Sounds like somethin's botherin' you about it, boss."

"Nah, when I first heard about it, I didn't think anything of it, y'know? I thought, 'Well, it's a fantasy world, so having some mysterious item like that is cool,' and it actually got me hyped..."

"But not anymore?"

"Yeah. Because now, I've got some ideas... some bad ideas, actually, about items that claim to tell you the future."

Subaru's face screwed up as if he'd bitten into a bitter bug, and Petra and Garfiel exchanged glances.

Subaru had been living in this other world for nearly two years now, but honestly, there were far too many things here that disguised themselves as future predictions or guidance of fate, trying to manipulate people.

In Vollachia, Abel was hostile towards the 'Stargazers.' The 'Witch of Greed' they met at the sanctuary had handed Beatrice a blank book dictating how she should live. And above all, the 'Gospel' said to reach the hands of Witch Cultists was known to twist their very existence towards evil.

How was this interference, which bent destiny from the sidelines, any different from that Dragon Chronicle?

"I get the feeling that if something's said to show the future, people naturally tend to rely on it. Especially if it has a track record. But letting someone win small at first, then ruthlessly collecting big time later—that's a classic scam technique, y'know?"

They let you win in insignificant areas to lure you into making a convenient choice for them later.

Not knowing what they might make you choose in that crucial, big decision, it would be dangerous to trust too much that this tattletale of fate is something beneficial for you.

Thinking this, Subaru felt a sense of crisis regarding the Dragon Chronicle's prophecy itself—but...

"――――"

"Huh? You two, what's with those faces?"

Garfiel and Petra's eyes went wide at Subaru's expression of his concerns.

Though they were the younger ones, they were reliable advisors—one intuitive, one logical. It was rare for them to react so identically to a single question. He had expected one of them to tilt their head in confusion while the other offered a practical answer.

And to Subaru's puzzlement, the two spoke one after another:

"Man, how should I put this――"

"--I was just surprised that the idea of doubting the Dragon Chronicle's prophecy never even occurred to us."

"Huh, it never occurred to you?"

"Yeah." "Uh-huh."

Both nodded simultaneously, and Subaru thought it must be some kind of joke. But their eyes were completely serious, and their expressions held strong bewilderment and confusion, making Subaru blink rapidly.

Their reactions felt like they'd encountered something truly unexpected, like being taught a completely unimagined solution to a quiz. This kind of cultural gap had been absent for a while—no, actually, he'd just experienced something similar recently regarding the succession of the throne by bloodline.

"I honestly thought that was a tragedy caused by me not being very familiar with a monarchical system where the king is supreme... but is this something deeper, like a matter of religious views?"

Customs differ with different climates, and common sense naturally varies. But conversely, with such fundamental differences in values, it was surprising that no major friction had occurred until now. He'd thought his otherworldly life was pretty tough, but apparently, it hadn't been the worst yet.

Also, the weight of this country being called the 'Dragonfriend Kingdom' suddenly became much clearer to him.

"Petra's a lovely maiden from a rural village, and Garfiel's a golden rookie who lived in a hidden hamlet cut off from the outside world."

In other words, they were people who hadn't received the education or benefits of being kingdom citizens to the same extent as those born and raised in the big city—yet even they didn't even conceive of doubting the Dragon Chronicle. This was proof that trust in the 'Dragon' had precipitated into the very depths of their unconscious minds.

If that was the case for these two, then those with a stronger identity tied to the kingdom would be even less likely to arrive at the idea of dismissing the Dragon Chronicle. ——That was precisely why this country had the foundation to implement the royal selection without hesitation, even in the absence of royalty.

If that were the case, then——

"--Lady Crusch's goal was seriously an incredible thing, huh."

Setting aside the rightness or wrongness of her ideals, he finally understood, with renewed appreciation, just how monumental a decision it was to advocate breaking away from the 'Dragon's' covenant in a world so permeated with trust in it.

In fact, going by pre-election reputation alone, Crusch had been the overwhelmingly strongest candidate in the royal selection. The very fact that the selection hadn't become entirely Crusch's race showed how difficult it was for her platform to be accepted in the current kingdom.

At the same time, Subaru also speculated about the true aim of the White Whale subjugation that occurred right after the selection began.

Of course, that subjugation must have also included Crusch's desire to let Wilhelm, whom she had taken into her camp, fulfill his revenge. But perhaps she also intended to demonstrate, by single-handedly defeating the White Whale—one of the Three Great Demon Beasts that had plagued people for centuries—her resolve and ideal: that she could lead the kingdom strongly without relying on the 'Dragon's' covenant.

――In the end, due to Subaru's intervention, the subjugation of the White Whale became known to the world as the result of a joint operation by three camps: the Emilia camp, the Anastasia camp, and Crusch's own camp.

Even so, Crusch probably didn't mind. Wilhelm had achieved his revenge, and despite facing headwinds from those around her, she had taken her first step towards fulfilling her ideals.

But then, those ideals of Crusch's were thwarted in an unexpected way――

"Am I an idiot? No, I *am* an idiot."

Having understood things to that point, Subaru finally keenly felt his own shallowness.

Without really understanding what the ideals Crusch had nearly had broken truly meant, he had visited her and, citing shallow empathy and pity, consoled her by saying he would help. Considering that, along with the tragic love story between Crusch and the Fourth Prince that Ferris had told him about at the mausoleum, just thinking about how insensitive and hollow his words must have sounded made him want to die of shame.

But――

"Hey, boss. I dunno if I should say this or not, but the way you go around shootin' your mouth off on impulse ain't exactly 'crystal fever,' is it——"

"We've been helped by Subaru's recklessness and daring before, so maybe... we'd prefer it if you didn't speak too badly of that trait."

"Huh!? That's exactly what I was about to say!"

"Sorry, I just wanted to score some好感度 (kōkando -好感度 points/favorability) with Subaru."

Sticking out her tongue playfully, Petra apologized cutely, causing Garfiel to deflate with a "Gao..."

Hearing their words, Subaru involuntarily bit the inside of his cheek hard. At first, he was surprised, and then he quickly didn't know what expression to make. He regretted the poor choice of words he'd used with Crusch, but he didn't want to make a sour face at Petra and Garfiel's consideration. The result was a half-formed, conflicted expression.

"...Don't spoil me too much like that, you two. If I start indulging, there'll be no end to it."

"Is that so? Well, I want to spoil Subaru more and more then. Right, Garf-san?"

"I dunno if 'spoil' is the right word, but the boss and Otto-nii are always lookin' out for others while hardly ever takin' care of themselves. Maybe I should give ya a good toss in the air once in a while."

"The last time you did that, Otto crashed through the ceiling..."

That was a tragedy caused by Otto, drunk after clearing a mountain of work, and Garfiel, whose excitement got the better of him, trying to cheer him on with all his might. Otto wasn't injured, and his memory was hazy due to alcohol, so it was a funny story now.

Anyway――

"I knew I had a lot I wanted to do and a lot I had to do... but it looks like I've added one more thing I need to check out."

With gratitude, Subaru reached out both hands and placed them on Garfiel and Petra's heads. As they were gently patted, looking a bit ticklish, Subaru narrowed his eyes at the soap bubbles floating and popping in the bucket.

There were plenty of people smarter and more knowledgeable than him. If he could find meaning in his existence, in his actions and thoughts, amidst such people――

"First things first, I want to properly understand what this Dragon Chronicle really is."

He wanted to believe that being able to doubt it—to question what power was at work, what will was guiding it towards which future—was the strength of Natsuki Subaru, the otherworlder.

First, start by learning. ——If he truly wished to stand by Crusch and Ferris, who were consumed by helpless frustration, in the way they truly needed, without being able to intervene.

However――

"...Thinking like this is probably exactly what Ferris would call arrogant, huh."


