Caught off guard by the exchange taking place in the kitchen, Rem found herself seriously debating whether to reach for the door or not.

"—It seems like we've stumbled right into the middle of a conversation that's a bit hard to barge in on."

"—Ah."

"Oh my? Did I startle you? If so, I'm sorry."

At Meili's words—the girl had suddenly peeked out from behind her—Rem shook her head, saying "No," while adjusting her grip on the water pitcher in her hands.

She had come to the kitchen to exchange the pitcher, now nearly empty, for a fresh one. Inside, Subaru and the others, tasked with cleaning up after dinner, were washing dishes in well-coordinated harmony while discussing matters related to the Royal Selection. Reluctant to interrupt their flow, Rem had decided to wait outside the kitchen until their topic reached a natural pause, listening quietly to their exchange.

"Don't you think Subaru-nii has a really troublesome disposition? The way he tries to shoulder everything himself, no wonder everyone ends up worrying about him."

When the conversation inside shifted to Subaru's nature—specifically his tendency towards excessive self-recrimination—Petra and Garfiel had immediately corrected and pointed it out, and Rem could tell Subaru was touched by their words.

It seemed Meili, standing behind Rem, could also vividly imagine the scene inside. She commented on it in her own way and was about to seek Rem's agreement when her eyes widened.

"Could it be... Rem-nee, are you really angry?"

"...I'm not angry, but I do have mixed feelings. It's just that I had said something very similar to what Petra-chan and the others just said to him, not too long ago."

"Ah, that would explain that face, I suppose."

What kind of face was she making? Meili's tone was cautious, as if treading on eggshells.

However, recalling the scene where she had conveyed something similar—Subaru at the Prison Tower, seemingly ready to shoulder even the responsibility for the death of the 'Gluttony' Archbishops he had killed—Rem hesitated to simply dismiss the issue as just Subaru's troublesome nature.

Because she knew that part of the reason for Subaru's way of thinking lay in her own attitude towards him.

"Honestly, the problem with Subaru-nii is that he seems to only count up his failures. It's really quite rude, you know."

As Rem, lost in such sentiments, lowered her eyes and tightened her grip on the pitcher, Meili, arms crossed, glared at the kitchen door and muttered.

"Rude?"

"Well, isn't it? I mean, even *I* think I've been helped by Subaru-nii before. Petra-chan and the others are the same, that's why they encourage him like that... so seeing him like that, isn't it just annoying?"

Meili puffed out her cheeks, pressing a finger to her lips. Seeing this childish display of sulkiness, Rem blinked several times, then unexpectedly let out a snort of laughter.

"Oh my, was that meant for me?"

"Sorry. I was just a little surprised. From my perspective, I thought both you and Petra-chan seemed quite mature for your ages."

"You mean cheeky? Well, mine is mostly just copying what I've heard, but Petra-chan's is a result of her own efforts to seem grown-up. So Emilia-nee and Rem-nee had better watch out, or she might just overtake you one day, and then you'll regret it."

"...I have no idea what you're talking about."

"Oh really? Fufufu, *really*?"

Suddenly, as if successfully getting back at Rem for treating her like a child, Meili covered her mouth with her hand and giggled triumphantly.

Letting out a small sigh at Meili's reaction, Rem looked once more towards the kitchen door.

"It seems like everyone in this camp has been saved by him at some point."

"Seems that way. That's probably part of why it's so hard to tell Subaru-nii 'no' when he tries to do something dangerous. –But, I don't think you need to worry about it so much."

"—?"

"Because really, I don't think there's anyone more cheeky and out of place here than me."

Pinching one of her own braids, Meili shrugged her shoulders. Catching the complex emotional tone in Meili's voice, Rem narrowed her light blue eyes.

"It's a bit late to say this now, but... not having my 'memories' is truly frustrating."

Meili clearly carried a significant burden.

Unlike with Emilia and the others, with whom she had deepened her bond in the Vollachia Empire, Rem's first meeting with Meili had been in the Kingdom of Lugnica, inside the dragon carriage Reinhard had transported them in.

From Subaru's rough explanation, she knew Meili had originally been an enemy, but his desire to avoid making things awkward had led to a rather vague account. In the end, she never learned the specifics of the conflict between them and Meili.

However, the complex emotion flickering across the girl's youthful face seemed like proof of hesitation stemming precisely from that past.

That was why—

"Please, don't be too hard on yourself either."

"—"

Meili's breath caught at Rem's words, and she stared intently at her. Without averting her eyes, Rem met that gaze head-on. Meili parted her lips slightly.

"Can you really say that? I mean, I've tried to kill you too, Rem-nee."

"—. Well, that is quite surprising, but... I don't remember it."

"Hmm, lucky me. ...I wonder if 'lucky' is really the right word, though."

Meili muttered, her voice faint, more like it was fading away than a whisper.

Fidgeting with her braid, she dropped her gaze from Rem to the floor.

"Honestly, I think it's all too good to be true. ...Getting a pass for what I did, being forgiven by the kingdom thanks to my Divine Protection... I keep thinking, maybe a huge payback is coming my way."

"Payback..."

"Like, if you do good, you get praised, and if you do bad, you get punished, right? I've been running away from that the whole time."

Guilt-ridden over being forgiven, Meili's words were pessimistic and painful.

Rem didn't know the specifics of Meili's deeds that caused her to think this way. She didn't even know whether to take the claim that Meili had tried to kill her as a joke or serious.

Perhaps, if she learned the full truth, Rem's own feelings might change. ——But for now, she didn't know.

And what she *could* say precisely because she didn't know right now, she felt, could only be said at this moment.

"I don't mean to compare, but I have similar worries."

"...Rem-nee? You, like me?"

"Yes. ...I don't consider myself so fortunate as to think that all I've received is something I'm entitled to."

What came to mind was her own behavior after waking up without her 'memories,' in an environment with no one to rely on—how she had been cold to him, to Subaru, and pushed away his helping hand.

The situation Rem was in back then was certainly harsh, but using that as an excuse only went so far. The gifts Subaru bestowed were by no means unlimited.

"I have to pay the price for that arrogance within me. Until that debt is settled, I don't even think I have the right to face him properly."

"—. You're hard on yourself."

"Am I? When I think of it as the payback for being hard on someone who... who cared for me, it seems only natural. But that's my story."

"—?"

Taking a step closer to Meili as she spoke, Rem hesitated for a moment, then gently placed her hand on the head of the puzzled girl.

And then—

"I don't think it's healthy for a child like you to be afraid of payback... so, if it comes, I'll surely fight against it for you."

"—Ah."

At Rem's faltering, carefully chosen words, Meili let out a tiny breath. Then she looked down, keeping Rem's hand on her head.

"—. Rem-nee, even without your 'memories,' I think you're every bit a part of this camp."

"...What do you mean by that?"

Rem frowned at Meili, who still hadn't looked up, asking what she meant. —And then.

"Kyah!"

Suddenly, Rem felt a strange sensation on the hand resting on Meili's head and let out an involuntary shriek.

Looking closely, a small creature had parted Meili's dark blue hair and peeked its head out. Recognizing its deep crimson, glossy sheen as belonging to a scorpion, Rem folded the arm holding the pitcher almost simultaneously—along with dousing Meili's head with the roughly half-pitcher of water remaining.

"Ah."

Both Rem, the perpetrator, and Meili, the victim, were dumbfounded at the scene. On top of Meili's wet head, the tiny scorpion, also soaked, clicked its pincers furiously in protest.

Despite being the surprise that caused this chaos, the scorpion itself was in a huff—how self-important. Before they could process the shock, the kitchen door flew open with a bang—

"Did I just hear Rem scream!?"

"...What terrible timing."

"Huh!? That's your reaction after I came running out of worry!? And what's going on here!?"

Subaru, bursting out upon hearing the scream, was surprised in stages. At his usual boisterousness, Rem was momentarily at a loss where to begin explaining.

But before she could, a laugh rang out: "Ahaha!" —It was Meili. Soaked from head to toe along with her scorpion, she was laughing.

"Hah, this is hilarious. No time to be moping around, I guess."

"Why are you so cheerful? Hey, Petra! Towel! Bring a towel!"

"Wow, Meili-chan is soaked! We need to get you changed right away!"

Petra came pattering over with a towel and began drying Meili's head, scorpion and all. As Meili passively accepted this, Rem, holding the now-empty pitcher, bowed her head.

Her attempt to show concern had ended in drenching the girl—the result couldn't be more backwards. But Meili waved her hand at the apologetic Rem, saying "Don't worry about it."

"Instead, I'll take what you said earlier seriously, okay."

"—"

Rem's eyes widened slightly at the look in Meili's eyes, visible through a gap in the towel. As this exchange happened, Petra, now behind Meili, pushed her shoulders.

"Okay, let's go get changed. Subaru, Garf-san, there's still cleanup left but..."

"Don't worry, we'll handle it. Make sure Meili doesn't catch a cold."

"Don't treat me like a child."

Leaving behind that sulky mutter, Meili was escorted away by Petra. Until the moment she disappeared down the hallway, Meili kept waving lightly towards Rem.

After seeing off the heartwarming sight of the two girls, Subaru looked at Rem with an "Um..."

"Oh, is that nee-sama's water? Well then, I'll just take that at least..."

"—. No, Petra-chan left because of me, so I'll help."

"Really? Then let's do it together."

Dodging his hand reaching for the pitcher, Rem offered to help Subaru. Looking slightly surprised, Subaru immediately agreed. At his prompt acceptance, Rem's eyes turned slightly narrow.

"...You sure agree to suggestions like this quickly."

"Huh? What do you mean?"

"It's nothing. Garf, I'm helping too, so move over."

"Well, that's helpful, but why's your way of addressin' me the only one that's so casual?"

"Because nee-sama called you that, and it just feels right."

Garfiel, with his hands in the bucket at the washing station, wrinkled his nose. Sensing Subaru wryly smiling nearby, Rem reflexively started to say something—but stopped.

Hadn't she just reflected on her own behavior while talking to Meili?

"What's up, Rem? You've got a cute face on, did something happen?"

"Huh?"

"Sorry, blurted that out! O-okay, come on, let's hurry up and finish cleaning so we can take nee-sama her water!"

Subaru, trying to cover his slip-up with enthusiasm, made a show of rolling up his sleeves and zipped over next to Garfiel.

Following him with a sigh, Rem admonished herself as firmly as she could.

Until the debt for her past actions was settled, she wouldn't earn the right to face this frustrating feeling.

"It's still a long, hard road ahead. Katcha-san, Priscilla-san."

What kind of face would she show him when she finally felt she had earned that right?

Still without an answer, Rem silently addressed the objects of her affection and respect within her heart, and joined the dishwashing team as Petra's substitute.



